# Put a Ring of Item Preservation on a character's finger, identify it, and
# open the item description screen, which is where the game renders the ring's
# page. Read by tools/check_ring_preservation_page.sh, finding PA-08-F9 of
# bd inc-tek.8.8.
#
# WHY THE DESCRIPTION SCREEN AND NOT THE FILE. This finding changes prose and
# nothing else, so no behaviour moves and no behavioural check is possible.
# The honest oracle is therefore the page as the PLAYER reads it, not the page
# as lib/m_items.irh stores it: grep over the source would prove only that a
# file changed. Item::Describe prints the entity's Desc through DESC(eID)
# (src/Help.cpp:3405-3408), and Inventory Mode's 'x' puts that on screen
# (src/Managers.cpp:751-754), so this script drives the game to the same
# screen a player would reach and lets the check read the rendered words.
#
# WHY THE PACK IS IDENTIFIED. Item::Describe prints the entity's page only
# when the item isKnown(KN_MAGIC) (src/Help.cpp:3405). Wizard mode's
# "Identify Whole Pack" calls MakeKnown(0xFF) on everything the character
# carries (src/Debug.cpp:717-720), a worn ring included.
#
# WHY AN ORC BARBARIAN IS ENOUGH. The ring gates itself on nothing at all. It
# is a plain EA_GRANT that registers one TRAP_EVENT stati, so any character
# who can wear a ring reads the same page. tools/keys/chargen.keys makes an
# orc barbarian, and that is all this reading needs.
#
#   w y                    enter wizard mode and open its menu.
#   Item Acquisition       RIGHT*26 walks the glyph row to Rings: ItemGlyphs
#   RIGHT*26               (src/Tables.cpp:320-349) puts -AI_RING 26 steps
#                          along. tools/keys/ring-command-level.keys walks the
#                          same 26 to the same list.
#   @until "Ring of Item   the candidate list has no menu letters, so the
#     Preservation" DOWN   cursor is walked. Three rows show and the MIDDLE
#                          one is selected, so the @until stops one row EARLY,
#                          on the pass where the wanted row first appears
#                          below the selected one, so ONE more DOWN is needed
#   DOWN                   to put the cursor on it. Without that extra DOWN
#                          the session takes the Ring of Elemental Command
#                          (Water), which is the row above it -- and that is
#                          exactly how tools/keys/ring-command-level.keys
#                          reaches the Water ring. The check does not trust
#                          any of this: it reads the ring's name back off the
#                          description box's own title line.
#   ENTER m SPACE          take it -- a ring carries no plus, so the
#                          acquisition asks for no item level -- then put the
#                          cursor on the Left Ring slot and SPACE to wear it
#                          (src/Managers.cpp:873). The slot was empty, so
#                          nothing is pushed into the in-air slot and nothing
#                          needs stowing before the manager will close.
#   ESC w i m x            leave the manager, identify the pack, reopen the
#                          manager, put the cursor back on the Left Ring and
#                          press 'x', which describes what is on it.
#   DOWN*6 three times     the box shows about forty rows and scrolls one row
#                          per DOWN (src/TextTerm.cpp:537-540). This page is
#                          shorter than that, so the first dump already holds
#                          it whole; the later dumps cost nothing and would
#                          carry the page if it ever grew past the box.

@include chargen.keys

w
y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*26
@until "Ring of Item Preservation" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
m
SPACE
@dump:ring-worn
ESC
w
@choose "Identify Whole Pack"
i
m
x
@dump:desc-1
DOWN*6
@dump:desc-2
DOWN*6
@dump:desc-3
DOWN*6
@dump:desc-4
ESC
ESC
